Quick Book Revision

When the node to delete is already given, no traversal is needed. Connect its previous node to its next node using node.prev.next = node.next, and connect its next node back using node.next.prev = node.prev. Handle the first/last node using null checks, then set node.prev and node.next to null to completely disconnect it.

Time: O(1)
Auxiliary Space: O(1)



/*
Definition for a Node.

class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode prev;
    public ListNode next;

    public ListNode();
    public ListNode(int data);
    public ListNode(int data, ListNode prev, ListNode next);
};
*/

class Solution {
    public void deleteNode(ListNode node) {

        // If the node is null, there is nothing to delete
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        // If the node has a previous node,
        // connect the previous node to the next node
        if (node.prev != null) {
            node.prev.next = node.next;
        }

        // If the node has a next node,
        // connect the next node back to the previous node
        if (node.next != null) {
            node.next.prev = node.prev;
        }

        // Completely disconnect the deleted node
        node.prev = null;
        node.next = null;
    }
}
